---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { arc, education } from '../data/skills';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="How I went from shipping SwiftUI apps to owning release engineering for a 70+ microservice enterprise platform."
>
  <section class="shell pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-24">
    <SectionHeading eyebrow="About" title="The longer version" />

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_18rem]">
      <div class="prose-body max-w-2xl">
        <p>
          I'm a platform engineer in Bengaluru. I spend my days on the machinery
          between a merged pull request and a running production service —
          pipelines, environments, permissions, telemetry, and the revert button
          you hope nobody has to press.
        </p>

        <h2>I started on the other side</h2>
        <p>
          My degree specialised in iOS development, and my first job was mobile:
          a native SwiftUI iPad application that I built essentially on my own
          for a client engagement at TCS. It shipped in about two months, the
          enhancement queue went quiet, and I picked up the AWS side of the
          engagement because there was nothing else to do.
        </p>
        <p>
          That turned out to be the fork in the road. I liked the infrastructure
          problem more than the app problem, and asked to move.
        </p>

        <h2>What changed my mind about infrastructure</h2>
        <p>
          I joined the Platform Solutions unit expecting to build a mobile client
          for <strong>TCS Enterprise Manager</strong>, an enterprise ITSM/ITOM
          product. Budget realities moved me into a DevOps role instead, right as
          the unit decided to modernise how it delivered software.
        </p>
        <p>
          The starting position was bleak in an instructive way: 70+ microservices
          in SVN, a fixed weekly deployment window, no review gate, no automated
          evaluation of a merge, and a revert path that meant an hour of careful
          manual work. Two of us were asked to fix it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The thing I'd tell anyone doing similar work is that the tooling was
          the easy part. We spent the first stretch writing down what the delivery
          model actually <em>is</em> — what branches mean, what a merge has to
          prove, how environments get promoted, who may deploy where. Every
          pipeline after that was an implementation of that document rather than
          an improvisation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The change that mattered most wasn't the deploy pipeline. It was the
          revert pipeline. Once undoing a release took under thirty seconds
          instead of an hour, people stopped hoarding changes for the Thursday
          window and started shipping when things were ready. Making failure
          cheap is what made speed possible.
        </p>

        <h2>Where my head is now</h2>
        <p>
          Increasingly on the boundary between platform, security and reliability.
          The OSS licence audit I pushed for, the policy-as-code deployment
          permissions, the Security Specialty certification — those came from the
          same instinct, which is that a delivery system nobody can reason about
          is a liability no matter how fast it runs.
        </p>
        <p>
          I've also fallen properly in love with self-hosting. Running your own
          identity provider, reverse proxy and search cluster is the cheapest
          operational education available, and a good chunk of what I've deployed
          at work I broke at home first.
        </p>
        <p>
          I still write Swift, and I'm currently building the product's Flutter
          client — so the mobile half of my background hasn't gone anywhere.
          It just isn't the headline any more.
        </p>

        <h2>Outside the terminal</h2>
        <p>
          I read a lot, I maintain more services than I strictly need, and I'm
          the person on the team who volunteers for the on-call rotation because
          it's the fastest way to find out what your monitoring is actually
          missing.
        </p>

        <hr class="hair my-10" />

        <p>
          If you're hiring for platform, DevSecOps, SRE or cloud architecture
          work — or you just want to compare homelab notes —
          <a href={site.socials.email}>{site.email}</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="space-y-4 lg:sticky lg:top-24 lg:self-start">
        <div class="card p-5">
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em]" style="color: var(--c-text-faint);">Arc</h2>
          <ol class="mt-4 space-y-3">
            {arc.map((event) => (
              <li class="flex gap-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">
                <span class="shrink-0 font-mono font-semibold" style="color: var(--c-accent);">{event.year}</span>
                <span style="color: var(--c-text-dim);">{event.label}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-5">
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em]" style="color: var(--c-text-faint);">Education</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-medium" style="color: var(--c-text);">{education.degree}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs" style="color: var(--c-text-dim);">{education.specialisation}</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-xs" style="color: var(--c-text-dim);">{education.institution}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-xs" style="color: var(--c-text-faint);">
            {education.period} · {education.grade}
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
            {education.coursework.map((course) => (
              <li class="rounded border px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.68rem]"
                  style="border-color: var(--c-border); color: var(--c-text-faint);">
                {course}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-5">
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em]" style="color: var(--c-text-faint);">Elsewhere</h2>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm">
            <li><a href={site.socials.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color: var(--c-info);">GitHub ↗</a></li>
            <li><a href={site.socials.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color: var(--c-info);">LinkedIn ↗</a></li>
            <li><a href={site.socials.credly} target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color: var(--c-info);">Credly ↗</a></li>
            <li><a href={site.resumePath} style="color: var(--c-info);">Résumé (PDF)</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
